|-----------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----|
| runs the user's $SHELL when it is an acceptable one             | /bin/bash, else /bin/sh; the description's ${shell} is its name                                                                                     | plan 019 section 3.9 pins the shell |
| ${tmp} is os.tmpdir()/opencode, made at startup                 | os.TempDir()/craze, made (0700, if missing) before every command                                                                                    | the description promises the directory exists; the tool framework has no startup hook |
| a timed-out or aborted command is an ordinary result            | the same text, as an error result classed timeout or aborted; an aborted result's text starts with "Tool execution aborted" (processor.ts's words) and keeps the output | craze classifies every failed call (plan 019 section 3.5) |
| (no cap, so no note)                                            | a reduced timeout adds "The requested timeout of N ms is above the maximum of 600000 ms; the command ran with a timeout of 600000 ms." to <shell_metadata> | the result says so (plan 019 section 3.9) |
| a shell killed by a signal has no exit code                     | "exit code: 128+N" for signal N, as a shell reports it                                                                                              | the model reads why the command failed |
| SIGTERM on abort or timeout, then SIGKILL after 3 s             | the same, with SIGCONT alongside the SIGTERM so a stopped command acts on it; when the session closes, SIGKILL at once; a stop that lands between the pre-start check and launch taking the started command kills it at once, without the grace, since it has run for the width of that window and produced nothing. The shutdown has one deadline, set when it starts (5 s after the SIGTERM, 2 s after a close's SIGKILL): a leader that dies late does not restart the 2 s drain wait | a stopped command would otherwise wait out the grace; closing must not wait; the bounds (6.7 s, 2.7 s) must hold whenever the leader dies (plan 019 section 3.9) |
| live output after every chunk, the last 30,000 characters       | a snapshot of the redacted in-memory tail (up to 100 KiB) at most every 100 ms, and only when it changed; it lags the output by up to twice the longest provider key's length plus one byte (each of the two redaction passes holds back that length less one byte, the escape-sequence stripper at most three bytes) | plan 019 sections 3.5 and 3.8: progress is lossy, and a key split across reads must not show half of itself |
| output decoded as UTF-8 text                                    | the bytes as written, less escape sequences (below); the tail is cut at a line end or, for one overlong line, on a character boundary              | Go; the kept text is opencode's |
| the output's terminal escape sequences reach the model          | every CSI, OSC, DCS, SOS, PM and APC sequence, every other ESC sequence and every C1 control (a code point, U+0080-U+009F, never a raw byte) is stripped, by x/ansi's DEC parser, before the tail, the progress snapshots and the spill file see the output; C0 controls stay. A sequence still open after 256 bytes is abandoned and what follows is text again. The output is redacted before the stripping as well as after it | tokens; a key an escape sequence splits is still redacted, and one the stripping would break is caught first (plan 033 section 3.6, ansi.go) |
| the full output is always saved (the write cannot fail), with no size limit | tool_<call id> under <Home>/tool-output, mode 0600, written by a goroutine of its own that the pipe's reader never waits on and that holds no lock while it touches the file; when the file cannot be opened or written, falls some 8 MiB behind, or is not finished 1 s after the output ends (at once, when the session is closing), it is given up: its backlog is dropped, "The full output could not be saved." stands where the path would, and whatever was written is left unnamed for the seven-day sweep, never removed by name (a remove is one more call a stalled filesystem could hold, and the name may by then belong to another file). When the output was not read to its end (below), a finished file is offered as "Output saved to: <path>" then "The saved output holds only what was read of the output.", never as full | a stalled filesystem under <Home> must not hold the turn, Close or the TUI, nor stop the pipe being read (output.go says how) |
| the timeout runs from the spawn                                 | the timeout runs from the call's start, so the work before the command (the workdir check, making ${tmp}, the exec) is under it too; a start that has not finished by then is abandoned and the call is a timeout | a workspace or ${tmp} on a filesystem that does not answer must not hold the turn |
| (no cap)                                                        | the spill file stops at 100 MiB (maxSpillBytes); the output is still read to its end and its tail kept, and the notice reads "Output saved to: <path>" then "The saved output stops at 100 MiB; the rest was not saved." | a runaway command (`yes`) would otherwise fill the disk within the timeout |
| reads the output until the stream ends                          | once the command's process group is dead, reads what is left in the pipe for at most 0.5 s; when something outside the group still holds it, <shell_metadata> says "The output may be incomplete: ..." | the output of a process that left the group has no end, and Run must return |
| a workdir that does not exist or is a file fails to spawn       | refused before anything runs: "workdir does not exist: <path>" (not_found) or "workdir is not a directory: <path>"                                 | a clear message instead of a spawn error |
| bash's environment is opencode's own process's                  | the environment the harness passes (craze's provider keys already gone), less OPENAI_*; with none passed, bash runs nothing: "The bash tool cannot run: no child environment configured." | the tool cannot know which variables hold provider keys, so it fails closed (plan 019 section 3.8) |
| PWD is opencode's own                                           | PWD is the working directory                                                                                                                        | what exec.Cmd does itself when it builds the environment |
| the user's pager, editor and terminal settings reach the command | overridden: PAGER, GIT_PAGER, MANPAGER and GH_PAGER are cat, AWS_PAGER and SYSTEMD_PAGER empty; GIT_EDITOR, GIT_SEQUENCE_EDITOR, EDITOR and VISUAL are true; GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0, GIT_ASKPASS and SSH_ASKPASS empty, SSH_ASKPASS_REQUIRE=never; DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive, TERM=dumb, NO_COLOR=1, FORCE_COLOR=0, CLICOLOR=0; CRAZE_AGENT=1 | a command has no terminal and stdin on /dev/null, so a pager, an editor or a password prompt would wait for the timeout (plan 033 section 3.6) |

Smaller differences found porting grep and glob:

| opencode                                                        | craze                                                                                                                                               | why |
|-----------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----|
| ripgrep comes from opencode's own binary service                | rg found on PATH, once per session (grep and glob share the lookup); without it both return "ripgrep (rg) is not installed or not on PATH. Use the bash tool with grep or find instead." | plan 019 decision 3: no download code |
| rg runs with opencode's environment and no time limit           | rg runs as bash's command does: its own session, the environment bash gives a command (craze's provider keys removed), killed with its group on a cancel, and SIGKILLed at once when the session closes; after 20 s it is stopped with "<grep or glob> tool terminated ripgrep after exceeding timeout 20000 ms. Consider using a more specific path or pattern." (class timeout) | a search must not hold a turn, or outlive it (plan 019 section 3.9) |
| grep shows a match's line with rg's line terminator, so every "  Line n:" line is followed by a blank line, and a CRLF line keeps its "\r" | the terminator ("\n" or "\r\n") is dropped                                                                                   | the blank lines only cost tokens |
| grep cuts a line at 2000 UTF-16 units, then "..."               | at 2000 runes (Unicode code points), never inside a character                                                                                       | Go semantics, as for read |
| a line or path that is not valid UTF-8, which rg reports as base64 bytes, fails the whole grep: "Invalid ripgrep match output" | the bytes are decoded: the line shows U+FFFD for each invalid byte, the path keeps its bytes                                      | one Latin-1 file must not break every search that reaches it |
| a line is shown as rg printed it                                | craze's provider keys are redacted from it before the 2000-rune cut                                                                                 | a key the cut would halve must not leave its first half behind (plan 019 section 3.8) |
| exactly 100 matches (or files) are reported as truncated: "(more matches available)" and the truncation note | only when rg printed a 101st                                                                                                          | exactly 100 is all of them; the note would send the model to narrow a complete answer |
| a grep path that is a file searches the whole directory holding it (grep.ts runs rg in that directory and does not pass the file to Ripgrep.grep) | that file alone, searched by name from its directory; include does not filter it, as rg filters no file it is given by name | grep.test.ts's "supports exact file paths" intends the file; a sibling's matches would mislead |
| a grep path that does not exist searches its parent directory; a glob path that does not exist fails to spawn: "ripgrep execution failed" | refused before rg runs: "grep path does not exist: <path>" / "glob path does not exist: <path>" (not_found)            | a clear message; the parent's matches would mislead |
| glob refuses a file with "glob path must be a directory: <path>"; grep reads whatever the path names | glob refuses anything that is not a directory with those words; grep refuses a path that is neither a file nor a directory: "Path is not a regular file or a directory: <path>" | a FIFO named outright would be read until a writer came (plan 019 section 3.9) |
| a glob rg cannot parse (glob's pattern, grep's include) exits 2 with "error parsing glob", which opencode reads as a search with no rows: "No files found" | rg's message, as an error, as opencode already does for a regex rg cannot parse                         | "No files found" would tell the model the files do not exist |
| an empty grep pattern fails when the call runs: "pattern is required" | refused before it runs, as invalid arguments: "... invalid arguments: pattern is required. ..."                                            | the input is checked before the gate |
| rg failing to start: "ripgrep execution failed", the cause kept out of the message | "ripgrep execution failed: <cause>"                                                                                                       | the model and the transcript see why |
| "\" in a path rg prints becomes "/"                             | kept                                                                                                                                                | a Windows rule; on Linux and macOS "\" is part of a name |
| glob reads `rg --files` a line at a time, so a name with a newline in it splits into two paths, and a file in a directory named "x\n.." reads as "../secret", joined onto the search's parent: a path outside the search, never searched | glob passes --null and splits rg's output on NUL, the one byte a name cannot hold. Every path rg prints, glob's and grep's (whose JSON escapes a newline and so could not split), must lie strictly under the directory searched once cleaned and joined, or the search fails: "Invalid ripgrep output: "<path>" is not a path under <dir>" | a path above the search must never be shown as found in it; rg never prints one, so one means its output cannot be trusted, and failing says so where dropping it would hide it |
| each path, or each file's header, is shown as it is, one a line | a path holding a control character (a newline, a tab, a carriage return) is shown Go-quoted: "/w/x\n../secret"                                  | otherwise a newline in a name reads as two paths, one of them outside the search |
| grep's card title is the pattern; glob's is the directory searched, relative to the worktree | both: the pattern, then " in <path>" when the call names a path, relative to the workspace                                           | one line that says what was searched for, and where; not model-facing |


